Fix xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland dumping core on every session teardown.

`g_pPortalManager` is a namespace-scope `std::unique_ptr<CPortalManager>`
(src/core/PortalManager.hpp). `main()` calls `init()`, which only returns once
the wayland connection is gone, and then returns 0 -- so `__run_exit_handlers`
runs the manager's (compiler-generated) destructor at exit. That destructor
destroys wayland proxy members, which marshal `destroy` requests onto a display
that no longer exists:

  #0  wl_map_insert_at              (libwayland-client)
  #1  proxy_destroy                 (libwayland-client)
  #2  wl_proxy_marshal_array_flags  (libwayland-client)
  #3  wl_proxy_marshal_flags        (libwayland-client)
  #4  CCZxdgOutputV1::~CCZxdgOutputV1()
  #5  Hyprutils::Memory::CSharedPointer<CCZxdgOutputV1>::_delete
  #6  std::unique_ptr<CPortalManager>::~unique_ptr()
  #7  __run_exit_handlers           (libc)
  #8  exit                          (libc)

A textbook static-destruction-order problem: a global outliving the connection
it depends on. There is no `~CPortalManager`, no signal handler anywhere in the
tree, and the event loop thread is never joined -- a second core-dump thread was
observed still parked in `CPortalManager::startEventLoop`'s condvar while the
main thread was already inside `exit()`, so the destructor races it too.

Reproduces 100% of the time on session teardown. On a greetd system, every
single login dumps core as the greeter session ends, and because the unit has
Restart=, systemd then logs

  xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland.service: Main process exited, code=dumped, status=11/SEGV
  xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland.service: Failed with result 'core-dump'
  xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland.service: Scheduled restart job, restart counter is at 1
  [CRITICAL] Couldn't connect to a wayland compositor

i.e. a guaranteed crash plus a pointless restart that immediately fails, on
every boot. Beyond the noise, it actively hampers debugging: a recurring
xdph SIGSEGV in the journal masks real crashes and has to be ruled out by hand
before any other portal fault can be trusted.

Fixed by releasing the global before returning, so the destructor never runs.
A deliberate leak-at-exit is the standard remedy here: the process is
terminating, the kernel reclaims everything, and the portal has no state to
flush. The more thorough fix -- marking proxies dead on disconnect so their
destructors skip marshalling -- is a much larger change across the wayland
wrapper layer and is not attempted here.

Note upstream already treats shutdown as unreliable: `CPortalManager::terminate`
forks a `sleep 5 && kill -9 <self>` watchdog rather than relying on a clean
exit.

Not upstreamed yet.

diff --git a/src/main.cpp b/src/main.cpp
index e5c3ed3..e3514ed 100644
--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -41,5 +41,20 @@ int main(int argc, char** argv, char** envp) {
 
     g_pPortalManager->init();
 
+    // init() only returns once the wayland connection is gone -- which is
+    // exactly when destroying the manager is unsafe. g_pPortalManager is a
+    // namespace-scope unique_ptr, so returning here lets __run_exit_handlers run
+    // its destructor, and the CCZxdgOutputV1 (and sibling) members marshal wl
+    // destroy requests onto a display that no longer exists:
+    // wl_proxy_marshal_flags -> proxy_destroy -> SIGSEGV in wl_map_insert_at.
+    // Reproduces on every session teardown; logging out of a greeter session
+    // dumps core every time, which then trips Restart= and leaves a unit that
+    // immediately fails with "Couldn't connect to a wayland compositor".
+    //
+    // The event loop thread is never joined either, so running the destructor
+    // here races it as well. Deliberately leak instead: the process is exiting,
+    // the kernel reclaims everything, and there is no state to flush.
+    (void)g_pPortalManager.release();
+
     return 0;
 }
